📗
  🖋 Hashes, signatures and the encodings they travel in. Every call is a type
  method: nothing is kept between one call and the next.
📗
🌍 🐇 🖋 🍇
  💭 The SHA-256 of *first* and then *second*, as if they were one message. *first*
  💭 has to be a whole number of blocks, which nothing and a block of 64 bytes are. A
  💭 signature hashes a block of its own in front of the message, and this spares it
  💭 a copy of the message.
  🐇 🔒❗️ 🔨 first 📇 second 📇 ➡️ 📇 🍇
    🆕🥣❗️ ➡️ bowl
    📏 first❓ ➡️ lead
    📏 second❓ ➡️ size
    0 ➡️ 🖍🆕at
    🔁 at ➕ 64 ◀️🙌 lead 🍇
      🥣 bowl first at❗️
      at ➕ 64 ➡️ 🖍at
    🍉
    0 ➡️ 🖍at
    🔁 at ➕ 64 ◀️🙌 size 🍇
      🥣 bowl second at❗️
      at ➕ 64 ➡️ 🖍at
    🍉
    💭 What is left of the message, the bit that marks its end, zeros, and its length in
    💭 bits as eight bytes, highest first: one block, or two when the length no longer
    💭 fits into the first (FIPS 180-4, 5.1.1).
    🆕🧳❗️ ➡️ tail
    📇 tail 🔪🐇🧮 second at 🤜size ➖ at🤛❗️❗️
    💧 tail 0x80❗️
    🔁 ❎ 🤜📐 tail❗️ 🚮 64🤛 🙌 56❗️ 🍇
      💧 tail 0❗️
    🍉
    🤜lead ➕ size🤛 ✖️ 8 ➡️ bits
    🔂 i 🆕⏩ 0 8❗️ 🍇
      💧 tail 🤜bits 👉 🤜56 ➖ i ✖️ 8🤛🤛❗️
    🍉
    📤 tail❗️ ➡️ last
    0 ➡️ 🖍at
